{
  "result": "At dawn, the city briefly forgets its arguments. Before engines begin their daily debate, before doors slam and phones fill with urgent rectangles, there is a quiet interval when the world seems willing to start over.\n\nA baker lifts a metal shutter, and warm air escapes into the street like a secret. Somewhere, a bus sighs at the curb. A pigeon struts beneath a parked bicycle, completely certain that the pavement belongs to it. A child in a yellow coat points at the moon, still visible above the rooftops, and her father looks up, not because he has forgotten what it is, but because she has reminded him.\n\nThis is one of the small miracles: attention can be borrowed. A child lends wonder to an adult; a stranger holding an elevator door lends patience to everyone behind them; a song leaking from an open window lends courage to someone walking alone.\n\nWe often imagine that change arrives with sirens, banners, and official announcements. Yet much of life is altered by nearly invisible offerings: a cup of water, a thoughtful question, a message sent before pride can stop it.\n\nThe future may be built by grand decisions, but it is inhabited through these modest gestures. When the afternoon grows loud and the headlines become heavy, we can still choose what to place in the world. We can leave a light on. We can make room at the table. We can answer gently. We can notice the moon before disappearing.",
